<h2>The two-motion architecture of Section 13B</h2>
<p>Section 13B dissolves a marriage on three cumulative findings: the spouses have lived separately for a year or more, they have not been able to live together, and they have mutually agreed to dissolve the marriage. Procedure mirrors substance. The first motion presents the joint petition; after a waiting period of six months (which courts may waive in appropriate cases) and within eighteen months, both parties must move the second motion; the court then makes such inquiry as it thinks fit and passes the decree.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>First motion.</strong> Joint petition; statements recorded; settlement usually exhibited.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Interregnum.</strong> Six to eighteen months — the window for reflection, and the window for second thoughts.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Second motion.</strong> Both parties reaffirm consent; the court verifies that consent subsists and is free.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Decree.</strong> Only here does the marriage end. Until this point, consent remains revocable.</div>
</div>

<h2>What the Supreme Court settled</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>Sureshta Devi (1991)</h4><p>Mutual consent must continue till the decree. A decree passed despite one party\'s withdrawal cannot stand — the Court set aside a divorce granted after the wife resiled. The judgment also defined "living separately" as the absence of the marital relationship, not of a common roof.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Hitesh Bhatnagar (2011)</h4><p>Consent may be withdrawn at any time before the decree; the 18-month outer limit exists to speed disposal, not to cut off withdrawal. Where the second motion is never jointly made, no decree can follow.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The combined effect is that Section 13B confers no vested right at the first motion. The petition is an offer renewed, not a contract concluded; the court\'s decree, not the settlement deed, dissolves the marriage.</p>

<h2>The hard case: withdrawal after taking benefits</h2>
<p>The rule protects genuine second thoughts, but it is sometimes used tactically — consent withdrawn after alimony instalments are pocketed or possession of property taken. The marriage cannot be dissolved against the resiling spouse under Section 13B, yet the conduct is far from free of consequence:</p>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Restitution of benefits.</strong> Amounts paid and property transferred under a failed settlement can be pursued for refund, and courts dealing with the parties\' later litigation take note of unjust retention.</li>
    <li><strong>Undertakings to the court.</strong> Where settlement terms and undertakings were recorded judicially — as in mediated settlements — breach can invite proceedings for violating the undertaking, a lever absent in purely private deeds.</li>
    <li><strong>The contested route revives.</strong> Withdrawal returns the parties to adversarial litigation, where the original grounds — cruelty, desertion — remain available, now supplemented by the record of the aborted settlement.</li>
    <li><strong>Credibility costs.</strong> In maintenance, custody and quashing proceedings between the same parties, a demonstrated pattern of resiling shapes the court\'s discretion.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2>Drafting against the risk</h2>
<p>Because consent is revocable by design, protection lies in sequencing, not in clauses purporting to make consent irrevocable — such clauses cannot override the statute. Settlements are therefore structured so that substantial payments and transfers track the second motion and the decree rather than preceding them; obligations are made expressly contingent on the decree being passed; quashing of criminal cases and withdrawal of other litigation are timed with the matrimonial milestones; and every term is recorded before the court so that undertakings attach. A well-drafted settlement assumes the possibility of withdrawal and makes resiling unprofitable, which is the only discipline the law permits.</p>
<div class="note"><p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice. The handling of a resiling spouse depends on the settlement terms, the stage of proceedings and the forum.</p></div>
